{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And I shall dwell among them — according to all that I show you, etc.</b> I shall dwell among them to receive their prayer and their service, in that manner in which I show you My Shekhinah on the mountain — upon the cover between the two cherubim — together with the pattern of the Tabernacle and the pattern of all its vessels. For indeed the pattern of the Tabernacle teaches about the cherubim, which are seraphim standing above it (Isaiah 6:2), who are seen by the prophets — some of them in the Holy, and some of them in the Holy of Holies, in two sets joined by clasps so as to be one, for the service of their Creator. And so it is fitting that all of Israel's holy ones be joined to the multitudes, to understand and to instruct. And in the Holy of Holies He placed the Torah within a body overlaid with gold within and without, as they said of blessed memory: כָּל תַּלְמִיד חָכָם שֶׁאֵין תּוֹכוֹ כְּבָרוֹ אֵינוֹ תַּלְמִיד חָכָם (Yoma 72b) — any scholar whose inside is not like his outside is not a scholar. And upon that body, which is the Ark, He placed the cover, entirely of gold, indicating the image of Elohim, not joined to it at all. And likewise the cherubim, their faces toward one another, indicate the activity of bringing the intellect into existence and receiving it — and this while gazing at the Torah, as it says: אֶל הַכַּפֹּרֶת יִהְיוּ פְּנֵי הַכְּרֻבִים, and in this they spread their wings upward, as it says: אֹרַח חַיִּים לְמַעְלָה לְמַשְׂכִּיל (Proverbs 15:24), and through this a person is fashioned in the divine likeness, as it says: וְאֶל זֶה אַבִּיט (Isaiah 66:2)." "<b>And thus shall you make.</b> You — in order that I may dwell among you to speak with you and to receive the prayer and service of Israel, not as the matter was before the calf, as it says: בְּכָל הַמָּקוֹם כוּ׳ אָבֹא אֵלֶיךָ (Exodus 20:21)."], :book :exodus, :voice :sforno, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {0 "The verb יִתְעַשֵּׁת at the end is read as hitpa'el of עשה, 'to be fashioned/formed'; the object הָאֱלֹהִים taken as 'the divine likeness' (cf. צלם אלהים) rather than 'God' directly."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T16:27:16.662723069Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}